--TEST--
A `Core\Test` assertion loop whose own ledger grows past `[limits] memory` stops with the limit report, not an empty `FATAL: ` — `rule:errors/on-limit`, `rule:errors/an-allocation-past-the-ceiling-is-refused-in-front-of-itself`
--RUN--
test
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every assertion adds an entry to the ledger, so the ledger's own storage is
// what crosses the ceiling. The loop is in a `#[Test]`, because an assertion
// outside a test records nothing. The test still holds those bytes when the
// runner reports the limit, and the report's text is built past the ceiling.
use Core\Test;

final class LedgerTest {
    #[Test]
    public function aLongLoopOfAssertionsReachesTheCeiling(): void {
        int $i = 0;
        while ($i < 2000000) {
            Test::assertSame($i, $i);
            $i = $i + 1;
        }
        echo "finished\n";
    }
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The ceiling the ledger above crosses, and nothing else.
[limits]
memory = "16M"
--EXPECTF--
  LedgerTest
    ✗ aLongLoopOfAssertionsReachesTheCeiling %f ms
      the program exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of %d, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler, when an allocation of %d bytes was refused
help: raise `memory` under `[limits]` in nvs.toml, or call `Core\Config::set('memory', '32M')` in the program

  1 failed, 0 passed, 0 skipped, 0 flaky in %d ms
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
